  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/747077">Remark <span data-tag="747077">7.2.1.16</span>.</a> It follows from Proposition <a href="/tag/0B62B7" data-tag="0B62B7">7.2.1.14</a> that the class of $\infty $-topoi having the form $\Shv (\calC )$, where $\calC $ is a small $\infty $-category, is not substantially larger than the class of ordinary topoi. More precisely, every topological localization of $\calP (\calC )$ can be obtained by inverting morphisms between <em>discrete</em> objects of $\calP (\calC )$. It follows that there exists a pullback diagram of $\infty $-topoi </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { \Shv (\calC ) \ar [r] \ar [d] &  \calP (\calC ) \ar [d] \\ \Shv (\Nerve ( \rm {h} \it {\calC })) \ar [r] &  \calP (\Nerve (\rm {h} \it {\calC })) } \]
</div>
  <p> where the $\infty $-topoi on the bottom line are $1$-localic, and therefore determined by the ordinary topoi of presheaves of sets on the homotopy category $h\calC $ and sheaves of sets on $h\calC $, respectively. </p>
